            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Convert lengths to centimetres, use similar triangles to express the water radius in terms of its depth, then substitute into the cone-volume formula.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "For a partially filled vessel, $R,H>0$ and $0<h<H$. The water surface is parallel to the rim, so an axial cross-section gives similar triangles for the water cone and the full cone.\n\nIn centimetres, the vessel radius, vessel height and water depth are $100R$, $100H$ and $100h$ respectively. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{r}{100h}&=\\frac{100R}{100H}\\\\r&=\\frac{100R}{H}h.\\end{align*}$$\n\nA cone's volume is one-third of its circular base area times its perpendicular height. With $V$ measured in $\\mathrm{cm}^3$,\n$$\\begin{align*}V&=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2(100h)\\\\V&=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi\\left(\\frac{100R}{H}h\\right)^2(100h)\\\\V&=\\frac{10^6\\pi R^2}{3H^2}h^3.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $R$ and $H$ are fixed, $V$ varies directly with $h^3$. The power is $3$ and the numerical constant of proportionality is $k=\\dfrac{10^6\\pi R^2}{3H^2}$ when $V$ is measured in $\\mathrm{cm}^3$ and $h$ in metres.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
